3. PERFORMANCE PROBLEM TO SOLVE
RevOS currently exhibits the following performance characteristics.
Problem A: Slow browser refresh
When accessing the live RevOS website and clicking the browser Refresh button, the refreshed application generally takes approximately five seconds or longer to become available and usable.
The objective is to identify why the complete refresh lifecycle takes this long.
Investigate:
•	Initial HTML delivery.
•	JavaScript and CSS downloads.
•	React application initialization.
•	Authentication and session restoration.
•	Supabase database requests.
•	Application configuration retrieval.
•	Component rendering.
•	Initial page readiness.
Problem B: Cloudflare Proxy performance differences
The owner has conducted comparative tests with Cloudflare DNS Proxy enabled and disabled.
The observed result is that website refreshes appear slower when Cloudflare Proxy is enabled.
Cloudflare has already undergone an initial configuration optimization.
Do not assume Cloudflare is necessarily the cause.
Investigate the application-side delivery configuration and identify whether there are remaining issues involving:
•	Response caching.
•	Asset caching.
•	Cache-control headers.
•	Static asset versioning.
•	Origin response behaviour.
•	Resource loading.
•	Duplicate requests.
Do not modify Cloudflare configuration as part of this task unless a specific application-side requirement is identified and explicitly documented.
Problem C: Performance degradation immediately after deployment
After pushing application updates through GitHub Actions to Wasmer, the first browser refreshes can take considerably longer than normal.
Investigate:
•	Vite production build output.
•	Hashed asset generation.
•	HTML and asset version consistency.
•	Cache invalidation.
•	Browser caching.
•	Wasmer CDN caching configuration.
•	Deployment propagation.
•	Asset availability immediately after deployment.
•	GitHub Actions deployment sequence.
Determine whether the problem originates from the build, deployment, caching, asset delivery or application initialization.
Do not assume that a new Wasmer deployment automatically explains the delay. Establish evidence.
Problem D: Increasing application complexity
RevOS continues to evolve, with new business engines, workflows, analytics, AI capabilities and content functionality.
The application must remain performant as additional features are introduced.
The optimization must therefore establish a scalable performance architecture rather than solve only the current five-second refresh problem.
