5. NON-NEGOTIABLE ENGINEERING PRINCIPLES
Before conducting the optimization, follow these rules.
5.1 Preserve the existing architecture
Do not migrate the application to Next.js, another framework, or a new hosting architecture simply to improve performance.
Optimize the existing React + Vite + TypeScript architecture first.
Do not introduce SSR, SSG or another rendering architecture without demonstrating why it is necessary and documenting the migration implications.
5.2 Preserve business functionality
Do not redesign existing pages or change business logic unnecessarily.
Do not modify business calculation logic, AI prompts, assessment methodologies, scoring models or database business rules unless a specific performance problem requires it and the impact is understood.
5.3 Preserve database integrity
Do not delete, rename or modify existing Supabase tables, columns, relationships, policies or functions as a routine optimization measure.
If database changes are necessary:
•	Explain the performance problem.
•	Identify the affected schema.
•	Propose a migration.
•	Explain compatibility implications.
•	Obtain approval before applying destructive or breaking changes.
Preserve all existing Row Level Security policies.
5.4 Preserve authentication and security
Do not weaken Supabase authentication.
Do not disable session verification.
Do not bypass authorization checks.
Do not expose service-role keys or other privileged credentials in frontend code.
Do not cache personalized or authenticated data in shared CDN caches.
Preserve existing user access permissions and subscription restrictions.
5.5 Avoid unnecessary dependencies
Prefer native React, Vite, browser APIs and existing libraries.
Only introduce additional libraries when their benefits justify the additional bundle size, maintenance requirements and complexity.
5.6 Avoid speculative optimization
Do not modify components simply because they appear to be potential performance problems.
Use code inspection, browser profiling, build analysis and actual performance measurements to identify bottlenecks.
Every significant optimization must have a clear technical justification.
