6. WORKSTREAM 1: COMPREHENSIVE PERFORMANCE AUDIT
Before changing application code, conduct a comprehensive technical audit.
Inspect the following areas.
6.1 Repository architecture
Identify:
•	Application entry points.
•	React initialization sequence.
•	Routing architecture.
•	Main application layouts.
•	Shared components.
•	Page-level components.
•	State management architecture.
•	Context providers.
•	Global hooks.
•	Authentication providers.
•	Supabase client initialization.
•	Application-wide configuration loading.
Produce an application initialization dependency map.
Identify which operations must complete before the first page becomes usable.
Identify which operations can execute asynchronously.
6.2 Frontend bundle analysis
Inspect:
•	package.json.
•	vite.config.ts.
•	TypeScript configuration.
•	Build scripts.
•	Production build output.
•	Existing dynamic imports.
•	Existing lazy-loaded components.
•	Third-party dependencies.
Determine:
•	Total JavaScript bundle size.
•	Initial JavaScript bundle size.
•	Total CSS bundle size.
•	Largest individual JavaScript chunks.
•	Large third-party dependencies.
•	Duplicate dependencies.
•	Unnecessary imports.
•	Components included in the initial application bundle unnecessarily.
Identify opportunities for:
•	Code splitting.
•	Tree shaking.
•	Dynamic imports.
•	Route-level lazy loading.
•	Component-level lazy loading.
Do not create excessive small chunks that introduce additional network overhead.
6.3 React initialization and rendering audit
Investigate:
•	Unnecessary component re-rendering.
•	Expensive calculations during rendering.
•	Excessive useEffect execution.
•	Duplicate state.
•	Unnecessary context updates.
•	Large component trees.
•	Heavy synchronous operations.
•	Inefficient list rendering.
•	SVG and chart rendering costs.
•	Animation-related performance problems.
Determine whether components below the visible screen are being unnecessarily initialized.
Identify opportunities for:
•	React.memo.
•	useMemo.
•	useCallback.
•	Virtualization where appropriate.
•	Intersection Observer.
•	Deferred rendering.
•	CSS-based animations.
Do not apply memoization indiscriminately.
Only optimize components where there is an identifiable rendering cost.
6.4 Supabase authentication audit
Investigate the complete authentication initialization lifecycle.
The existing Supabase browser-managed session persistence must be preserved.
Inspect:
•	Supabase client initialization.
•	Session restoration.
•	Auth state listeners.
•	getSession calls.
•	getUser calls.
•	Authentication context.
•	Protected route initialization.
•	Permission retrieval.
•	User profile retrieval.
•	Duplicate authentication requests.
•	Authentication-related loading states.
Identify whether application initialization is unnecessarily waiting for multiple authentication requests.
Avoid repeatedly subscribing to authentication events.
Avoid duplicate Supabase client instances.
Avoid unnecessary getUser requests when a validated existing session is sufficient for the relevant operation.
However, preserve server-side authorization and security checks where required.
Optimize authentication initialization without weakening security.
6.5 Supabase data request audit
Inspect all significant frontend Supabase requests.
Determine:
•	Which requests happen during initial application loading.
•	Which requests are page-specific.
•	Which requests execute sequentially.
•	Which requests can run in parallel.
•	Which requests retrieve excessive data.
•	Which requests retrieve unused fields.
•	Whether requests are duplicated.
•	Whether data is fetched repeatedly during navigation.
•	Whether database queries are unnecessarily expensive.
Classify data requests into:
A. Critical initialization data.
B. Page-specific data.
C. Background and supplementary data.
Implement appropriate loading strategies for each category.
Independent requests should execute concurrently where appropriate.
Noncritical requests must not unnecessarily block the initial user interface.
Use pagination for large datasets.
Avoid retrieving complete tables when only a subset of records is required.
Do not compromise RLS or authorization to reduce query latency.
6.6 Asset delivery audit
Inspect:
•	Images.
•	SVG files.
•	Icons.
•	Fonts.
•	CSS.
•	JavaScript.
•	Video and other large resources.
Identify oversized or unnecessarily duplicated resources.
Determine whether assets are appropriately compressed, versioned and cached.
Check whether images are unnecessarily loaded before becoming visible.
Recommend appropriate lazy loading and responsive image strategies.
6.7 GitHub Actions and Wasmer deployment audit
Inspect:
•	All GitHub Actions workflow files.
•	Build commands.
•	Dependency installation.
•	Build artifact generation.
•	Deployment commands.
•	Wasmer configuration files.
•	Static asset delivery configuration.
•	Deployment verification.
Determine whether:
•	Dependencies are unnecessarily reinstalled.
•	Build operations are duplicated.
•	Unnecessary files are included in deployment.
•	Asset versioning is consistent.
•	The HTML entry point references the correct assets.
•	Deployment completion is verified.
•	The deployed application becomes available before the workflow reports success.
Investigate whether GitHub Actions can efficiently reuse dependency caches.
Do not sacrifice deployment reliability for a faster workflow.
