8. WORKSTREAM 3: SUPABASE AND APPLICATION INITIALIZATION OPTIMIZATION
This is a particularly important workstream.
8.1 Establish an optimized initialization sequence
Design an application initialization strategy that:
1.	Initializes the Supabase client once.
2.	Restores the existing authentication session.
3.	Establishes the authenticated user context.
4.	Retrieves only essential application configuration.
5.	Establishes the user's authorized application access.
6.	Renders the appropriate application shell.
7.	Retrieves page-specific data independently.
8.	Loads noncritical background information asynchronously.
Do not make the entire application wait for all database queries to finish.
8.2 Introduce appropriate request management
Where justified, implement:
•	Request deduplication.
•	Parallel requests.
•	Cancellation of obsolete requests.
•	Pagination.
•	Selective field retrieval.
•	Appropriate client-side caching.
•	Background data refresh.
If TanStack Query or an equivalent request-management library already exists, optimize its current configuration before introducing another library.
If no request-management library exists, first assess whether native React and existing application patterns are sufficient.
Do not introduce an additional library without justification.
8.3 Establish sensible data freshness policies
Different categories of data require different freshness requirements.
For example:
•	User permissions: retrieve and validate appropriately.
•	User-specific business records: use appropriate authenticated data access.
•	Public static content: cache where safe.
•	Application configuration: cache where appropriate.
•	AI-generated recommendations: use suitable freshness and persistence policies.
Never apply a single caching policy to every category.
Never cache personalized information in a public CDN cache.
