9. WORKSTREAM 4: VITE BUILD, CACHING AND ASSET OPTIMIZATION
9.1 Optimize Vite production configuration
Inspect and optimize vite.config.ts.
Investigate:
•	Production minification.
•	Tree shaking.
•	Chunk splitting.
•	Dependency optimization.
•	CSS optimization.
•	Asset naming.
•	Source map generation.
•	Build output configuration.
Use manualChunks only when there is a clear performance benefit.
Avoid generating excessively large initial bundles.
Ensure that the build output remains compatible with Wasmer static hosting.
9.2 Establish a production asset caching strategy
Implement the following intended cache policy where supported by the actual hosting and serving configuration.
HTML entry point:
Cache-Control: no-cache
Content-hashed JavaScript:
Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
Content-hashed CSS:
Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
Public versioned images:
Use an appropriate long-term cache policy.
Authenticated and personalized responses:
Cache-Control: private, no-store
Important:
Inspect how Wasmer serves static files and whether the relevant HTTP response headers can actually be configured.
Do not assume that modifying a configuration file automatically changes Wasmer response headers.
Do not introduce a custom server simply to set headers unless necessary.
9.3 Preserve deployment version consistency
Ensure that every production deployment:
•	Generates a consistent Vite asset manifest.
•	Generates the correct index.html.
•	References the corresponding JavaScript and CSS files.
•	Deploys all required assets.
•	Avoids references to nonexistent assets.
•	Preserves appropriate cache policies.
•	Does not unnecessarily invalidate unchanged immutable assets.
Ensure that old hashed assets are not prematurely removed when they might still be referenced by existing browser sessions.
Investigate Wasmer's CDN caching configuration and its interaction with Cloudflare.
Do not blindly enable additional caching layers.
