11. WORKSTREAM 6: PERFORMANCE MONITORING AND ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
Establish a measurable performance baseline.
Use available tools such as:
•	Chrome DevTools.
•	Lighthouse.
•	Lighthouse CI, where practical.
•	Vite build analysis.
•	Browser Performance API.
•	Supabase request timing.
•	GitHub Actions execution logs.
11.1 Core Web Vitals targets
Use the following as initial engineering targets:
Metric	Target
First Contentful Paint	≤ 1.8 seconds
Largest Contentful Paint	≤ 2.5 seconds
Interaction to Next Paint	≤ 200 ms
Cumulative Layout Shift	≤ 0.1
Total Blocking Time	Ideally < 200 ms
Distinguish actual field metrics from laboratory measurements.
Do not claim that laboratory tests establish real-user Core Web Vitals.
11.2 RevOS-specific performance targets
Establish the following objectives:
Measurement	Initial target
Normal browser refresh	≤ 2 seconds
Initial application usability	≤ 2.5 seconds
Initial HTML TTFB	Ideally ≤ 500 ms
Initial JavaScript transfer	Reduce materially from baseline
Duplicate initialization requests	Eliminate unnecessary duplicates
Deployment-related performance regression	No avoidable regression
These are engineering objectives, not guaranteed results.
If network conditions, external services or hosting limitations prevent a target from being achieved, identify the limiting factor and document the evidence.
11.3 Define application usability
Do not measure performance only by whether HTML has arrived.
Define when RevOS is actually usable.
For an authenticated user, the application should be considered ready when:
•	The correct application shell is displayed.
•	Authentication has been restored.
•	The appropriate user permissions have been established.
•	The requested page's critical content is available.
•	The main interface is interactive.
Noncritical background data must not prevent this state.
Instrument the relevant lifecycle events using appropriate performance marks.
